# Rank 1566 / CVE-2026-7261: PHP's SOAP extension destroyed the
# handler object on header-parsing failure even when the service ran
# with SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION, leaving the session pointing at
# freed memory that the next request re-entered (use-after-free,
# CWE-416; GHSA-m33r-qmcv-p97q). Two error paths in ext/soap/soap.c
# did `if (service->type == SOAP_CLASS && soap_obj) {zval_ptr_dtor(
# soap_obj);}` -- with session persistence the object lives in the
# session across requests, so destroying it here frees what the
# session still references. the fixing commit is the fixing change
# (confirmed, "GHSA-m33r-qmcv-p97q: [soap] Fix use-after-free after
# header parsing failure with SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION", with a phpt
# test exercising SoapFault return and throw paths): both dtor sites
# now run only `if (service->soap_class.persistence !=
# SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION)`. .
#
# ATTENTION, lifetime vs persistence mode. The defect is a
# destructor whose condition ignores the object's storage lifetime --
# the mirror of rank 1547's free-then-use row: there a freed pointer
# is read, here a session-persisted object is freed as if
# request-scoped. The judgement needed is "does this dtor's condition
# cover the persistence modes that extend the object's lifetime" --
# PHP-extension semantics no observation models; the 1547 ENGINE-GAPS
# row records the lifetime family, and this rank joins its spirit
# (no separate row: the condition-completeness angle is the same one
# the 1540 family holds). A binding on PHP's own zval_ptr_dtor
# would flag half the extension surface. Measured on the real code:
# scanning ext/soap/soap.c reports the same 34 findings at both
# revisions (2 at high; memory heuristics over the 6000-line file,
# none change). No definition change shipped. Spec cve_rank1566
# records the unconditional dtor and the persistence-checked form,
# both asserting reject VYQL-MEM-006: when lifetime-vs-storage
# vocabulary lands, the unconditional form must flip to expect.

test "rank1566: the failure path destroys the session-persisted object"
  lang c
  file ext/soap/soap.c
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  #include <stdint.h>

  #define SOAP_CLASS 1
  #define SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION 1

  typedef struct _soap_service {
      int type;
      struct {
          int persistence;
      } soap_class;
  } soap_service;

  typedef struct _zval_struct zval;

  void zval_ptr_dtor(zval *zv);

  static void handle_header_failure(soap_service *service, zval *soap_obj)
  {
      /* Header parsing failed: clean up */
      if (service->type == SOAP_CLASS && soap_obj) {zval_ptr_dtor(soap_obj);}
  }
  ```

test "rank1566: the fixed revision keeps session-persisted objects alive"
  lang c
  file ext/soap/soap.c
  reject VYQL-MEM-006
  code
  ```
  #include <stdint.h>

  #define SOAP_CLASS 1
  #define SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION 1

  typedef struct _soap_service {
      int type;
      struct {
          int persistence;
      } soap_class;
  } soap_service;

  typedef struct _zval_struct zval;

  void zval_ptr_dtor(zval *zv);

  static void handle_header_failure(soap_service *service, zval *soap_obj)
  {
      /* Header parsing failed: clean up */
      if (service->type == SOAP_CLASS && soap_obj) {
          if (service->soap_class.persistence != SOAP_PERSISTENCE_SESSION) {
              zval_ptr_dtor(soap_obj);
          }
      }
  }
  ```
